  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-704e9646 Web logs overview\"**\n- ID `eff-704e9646-web-logs-overview-28fdac26a733`, six panels on the `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view:\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n  - Total bytes served (sum of `bytes`)\n  - Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n  - Responses by status code (bar, terms on `response.keyword`)\n  - Top requested URLs (table, terms on `url.keyword`)\n- Time range is set to the last 5 years so the sample data shows up regardless of when it was loaded. Adjust it if you want a shorter window.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-704e9646 5xx spike\"**\n- ID `6e5151e4-fedf-4101-8063-55e94320b9a6`, type `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`, runs every 1m, enabled, status `ok`.\n- Condition: count of documents on `kibana_sample_data_logs` where `response.keyword` matches `5*`, over a 5-minute window, fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- I enabled it so it actually runs. The tool creates rules disabled by default, so say if you want it off.\n- No connector is attached, so the rule will record firings but won't send notifications.\n\n**Limitations**\n- I could not run aggregations against the data. Elasticsearch isn't reachable from this environment (`localhost:9200` refused connections), and Kibana's console and ES|QL proxies are disabled. So I haven't confirmed that the data contains any 5xx responses, or what the status-code values look like. The rule's wildcard assumes three-digit string codes, which is the usual format.\n- I read the field list from `describe_data_view` only, not from the documents.",
